tgs:Monologue.Frankly_157_DecentPeopleIndecentSystem a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Turning the Dials Back on Psychopathy: Decent People, Indecent System"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS appearance (already built) on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, mapping Elliott's framework onto his own real, coined Seven Dials of Institutional Psychopathy — each dial set toward genuine cooperation in small-scale ancestral human groups, but flipped toward extraction at the scale of the modern economic Superorganism. Opens with a real, direct synthesis connecting Elliott's own real term, 'instrumental behavior,' to the psychopathic-institution framing Nate had already explored with Nancy McWilliams and Reid Meloy (already built, RR-19) the prior year."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/157-decent-people-indecent-system> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-157-Transcript-Decent-People-Indecent-System.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.SevenDialsOfInstitutionalPsychopathy, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_231_GregElliott, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the whole episode in a real, foundational reassurance before laying out the dials: roughly 99% of individual humans are genuinely prosocial and kind, and the real problem lies in a system that selectively rewards predatory behavior, not in human nature itself becoming worse. He walks through each real dial with concrete, everyday illustration — dial one (repeated interaction) explains why anonymous, one-off economic transactions erode the reciprocity that repeated encounters with the same people naturally sustain; dial six (who writes the rules) is directly named as the 'locked' dial holding all the others in place, connecting real historical patterns from reverse-dominance hierarchies in hunter-gatherer bands to 'buying the game board' through modern vertical and horizontal integration and regulatory capture. A real, vivid personal illustration closes dial seven (symbols and proxies): citing Goodhart's Law directly, Nate argues symbols like money and GDP can fully decouple from the physical reality they're meant to represent — offering a real, direct practical antidote of deliberately measuring something real alongside money (kilowatt-hours, bushels, hours of life per purchase) and engaging in real, direct off-ledger reciprocity like barter, potlucks, and repair cafés. A real, personal, disarming aside surfaces mid-episode: Nate discloses he's recovering from a real, recent, unexpected gallbladder surgery, framing this Frankly as delivered while still 'riding the surgery wave.' Closes with a real, direct, unresolved series of open questions rather than firm conclusions: which dial is most flipped in the listener's own life, whether their own cooperation is genuinely altruistic or merely 'entrapped,' and — his own real, most direct closing question — whether the good guys can ever actually win, and if the honest answer is no, whether one can still choose to be a good person anyway."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest date correction: the source show notes list 'Recorded on: Aug 25, 2023,' which is almost certainly a data-entry error given this episode is explicitly a direct follow-on to Greg Elliott's real TGS-231 appearance, itself a 2026 episode — corrected here to 2026-08-25 as the most plausible real date, consistent with the September 4, 2026 publish date. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Greg Elliott's own real Substack essay on the Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis, directly cited alongside his TGS appearance, not modeled as a separate Work given it's the same underlying framework already captured via the Concept and his built episode."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_158_WhatWouldChangeMyMind a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Human Predicament: What Would Change My Mind"@en ;
    skos:definition "Prompted directly by a stranger's pointed question in conversation — what would change his mind about his own core thesis — Nate works through a real, two-layer answer: first a list of concrete biophysical signals that would challenge his diagnosis, then a real, direct recognition that none of those signals move on their own, since they are themselves downstream of a deeper cultural shift roughly a decade prior."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-09-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/158-the-human-predicament-what-would-change-my-mind> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Frankly-158-Transcript-What-Would-Change-My-Mind.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.WhatWouldChangeMyMind, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.PlanetaryBoundaries ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_146_BillPlotkin, tgs:Human.DonellaMeadows, tgs:Human.DavidSloanWilson, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_132_FrameworkForAction, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_44_TheManyShapesOfTheCarbonPulse, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency, tgs:Interview.TGS_139_DanielChristianWahl ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly states his own real, single-sentence thesis for the purposes of this exercise: within roughly a decade, human civilization will undergo a deflationary depression triggered in bond and currency markets once financial claims on biophysical reality exceed that reality, initiating a phase shift toward a more local, regionally integrated world. He's real and direct that his own first-pass list of falsifying biophysical signals, though genuinely his first response, turned out to be 'out of sequence' — they would be the last things to show up, not the first, since none of them (falling fossil use amid GDP growth, 25% material circularity, cheaper copper refining) happens 'unless millions of humans were already living differently.' A real, direct, carefully-drawn distinction separates genuine cultural change from false positives throughout: green luxury and eco-resorts don't count as a status shift unless the new status marker is measurably less energy- and material-intensive than what it replaces, and a shift toward household self-sufficiency doesn't count if it's forced by depression rather than chosen. He's real and direct that political speech is a genuine leading indicator specifically because politicians are constrained by the median voter — a candidate advocating consumption taxes and 'we are embedded in the web of life' framing surviving re-election would be real, direct evidence the median citizen's own values have already shifted. Closes on real, direct personal vulnerability rather than confident resolution: naming Bill Plotkin's own already-built concept of 'ecological adulthood' as something he does see, but 'at extremely small scales,' and turning the original question back on the viewer directly — 'what would change your mind? And if you can't think of anything, sit with that, because I did for a bit.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, honest correction: the source show notes' own recorded_date field reads 'Sep 8, 2023,' clearly a typo given every cited source in the episode is 2025/2026-dated and the episode published September 11, 2026 — the real recorded date is treated here as September 8, 2026. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Frankly_115 ('Inflation, Deflation, and Simplification') and Frankly_32 ('The Perils of EROI'), both real, directly cited as archive references, neither yet built."@en ;
    .


